The parent is re-rendering, which makes all its children re-render too. React does this by default even if the props didn't actually change.

Three common reasons this happens:

**Your parent's state changed.** Even an unrelated state update in the parent will re-render the child. The fix is wrapping the child with `React.memo` so it only re-renders when its props actually differ.

```jsx
export const Child = React.memo(({ name, age }) => {
  return <div>{name}: {age}</div>;
});
```

**The props are new objects or functions each time.** If the parent does `<Child items={[1, 2, 3]} />` or `<Child onClick={() => alert('hi')} />`, that's a fresh reference every render, so the child sees "new" props. Move these outside the component or wrap them with `useMemo` and `useCallback`.

```jsx
// Before: new array every render
<Child items={[1, 2, 3]} />

// After: same array every time
const ITEMS = [1, 2, 3];
<Child items={ITEMS} />
```

**You're not actually memoizing.** If you did add `React.memo` but the issue persists, check that you're also memoizing any function or object props the parent passes down.

The simplest fix for most cases is wrapping your child in `React.memo` and making sure its props are stable references.
